## Why This Is Asked
Stack growth is a core OS feature. This question tests understanding of page faults, per-thread state, synchronization, and safe growth under concurrent faults in a minimal kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Guard pages and dynamic stack growth
- Per-thread stack boundaries (stack_base, stack_limit)
- Atomic/locked updates to stack metadata
- Race conditions when multiple faults occur near the guard
- Basic testing: concurrent faults, growth cap, and resume correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-skeleton: handle_stack_fault in a toy OS
void handle_stack_fault(Thread *t, void *fault_addr) {
  if (!in_stack_range(t, fault_addr)) return; // not stack fault
  acquire(&t->stack_lock);
  if (t->stack_limit - t->stack_base < GROW_SIZE) {
    release(&t->stack_lock);
    // terminate or signal OOM
    return;
  }
  map_pages(t, t->stack_limit - GROW_SIZE, GROW_SIZE); // grow downward
  t->stack_limit -= GROW_SIZE;
  release(&t->stack_lock);
  resume_execution(t);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to prevent double-growth when two faults race on the guard page?
- What testing harness would you use to verify correctness and latency under contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for dynamic participant counts?
- How would you test for spurious wakeups and ensure progress under contention?

